q('A person with advanced HIV develops progressive focal neurological deficits and asymmetric nonenhancing white-matter lesions with little mass effect. Which diagnosis best fits?', 'JC-virus-associated progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy',[
 ('Toxoplasma encephalitis','It more often produces enhancing mass lesions.'),('Primary CNS lymphoma as established by this pattern alone','The characteristic demyelinating pattern favors PML, though atypical cases require assessment.'),('Cryptococcal meningitis as the only possible cause','It more often produces a meningitic syndrome rather than this characteristic pattern.')],
 'PML damages myelin-forming cells', '**JC virus can reactivate with severe cellular immunodeficiency.**',['Symptoms commonly progress over weeks.','Inflammatory PML after immune recovery can enhance, so patterns are not absolute.'], '**Use CSF JCV PCR with the clinical and MRI findings.**',['A negative test does not exclude every case, especially with low viral burden.','Repeat sensitive testing or biopsy may be needed when suspicion remains high.'],[['Typical MRI','Asymmetric white-matter lesions'],['Usual mass effect','Limited'],['Confirmation','Clinical/MRI pattern and JCV evidence']],[PM],style='clinical_scenario')
q('What is the central treatment principle for HIV-associated PML?', 'Start or optimize effective ART to restore immune control',[
 ('Routine cidofovir as an established effective anti-JC cure','It has not demonstrated an established beneficial role.'),('Defer ART until all white-matter lesions disappear spontaneously','Immune restoration is the key intervention.'),('Give corticosteroids routinely to all noninflammatory PML cases','Steroids are reserved for selected clinically important inflammatory complications.')],
 'There is no established direct antiviral cure that replaces immune restoration', '**Effective ART is the main treatment strategy.**',['Evaluate resistance and adherence if the patient is already on therapy.','Avoid unsupported intensification when systemic suppression is already adequate without specialist rationale.'], '**Monitor for PML-associated IRIS.**',['Severe inflammatory edema may need carefully selected corticosteroid treatment.','Functional rehabilitation and supportive care are also important.'],[['Core therapy','Effective ART'],['Direct antiviral','No established curative substitute'],['IRIS','Assess inflammation and mass effect']],[PM])
q('A person with advanced HIV has oral thrush and painful swallowing. Which treatment approach is appropriate for likely esophageal candidiasis?', 'Systemic fluconazole when suitable, with evaluation of response and alternative causes if symptoms persist',[
 ('Topical oral nystatin alone as adequate esophageal therapy','Esophageal disease requires systemic treatment.'),('Valganciclovir solely because odynophagia proves CMV','Several causes produce odynophagia.'),('No treatment because oral thrush is always harmless','Esophageal symptoms require assessment and therapy.')],
 'Esophageal involvement needs systemic rather than topical-only treatment', '**Candida can cause odynophagia, dysphagia and retrosternal discomfort.**',['Oral thrush supports the diagnosis but can be absent.','A therapeutic trial is appropriate in suitable presentations.'], '**Investigate nonresponse or atypical features.**',['Endoscopy can identify CMV, HSV or resistant Candida.','Review azole interactions and immune-restoring ART.'],[['Oral-only disease','Topical options may be suitable'],['Esophageal disease','Systemic therapy'],['Nonresponse','Endoscopic and microbiological reassessment']],[CA],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A patient’s presumed Candida esophagitis does not improve with appropriate fluconazole. What is the best next principle?', 'Reassess with endoscopy and relevant sampling for resistance or alternative esophageal disease',[
 ('Increase the dose indefinitely without confirming the cause','This can delay diagnosis of a different process.'),('Assume every nonresponse proves ART failure','Local resistance and other diseases can occur.'),('Treat the absence of oral thrush as proof that the esophagus is normal','Esophageal disease can occur without oral lesions.')],
 'Persistent symptoms require diagnostic refinement', '**CMV and HSV can produce ulcerative esophagitis.**',['Resistant Candida is another possibility.','Medication injury and other noninfectious causes can mimic infection.'], '**Use tissue and microbiology appropriate to the lesion.**',['Choose subsequent therapy from the actual diagnosis.','Review adherence, absorption and interacting medicines as well.'],[['Possibility','Resistant Candida'],['Alternative','Viral or noninfectious esophagitis'],['Next step','Targeted endoscopic sampling']],[CA],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An advanced-HIV patient has prolonged profuse watery diarrhea due to Cryptosporidium. Which intervention is the treatment mainstay?', 'Effective ART with immune restoration, together with fluid, electrolyte and nutritional support',[
 ('Nitazoxanide as a uniformly curative substitute for immune restoration','Its efficacy in profound immunodeficiency is limited and it does not replace ART.'),('TMP-SMX as the reliably curative specific treatment','It is useful for some other intestinal coccidia, not standard curative Cryptosporidium therapy.'),('High-dose steroids to restore immunity','They can worsen immunosuppression.')],
 'Cryptosporidiosis can persist when cellular immunity is profoundly impaired', '**Immune recovery is central to sustained improvement.**',['Antiparasitic adjuncts may be considered but have limitations.','Severe stool losses can cause major dehydration and wasting.'], '**Provide active supportive care and prevention advice.**',['Monitor volume and electrolytes.','Address safe water and exposure prevention.'],[['Core','Effective ART'],['Support','Hydration, electrolytes and nutrition'],['Adjunct drugs','Do not replace immune restoration']],[CY],style='clinical_scenario')
q('An HIV-positive patient with persistent watery diarrhea has large elliptical acid-fast oocysts in stool. Which treatment is preferred for Cystoisospora infection?', 'Trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole',[
 ('Valganciclovir','It treats selected CMV disease, not this parasite.'),('Metronidazole as the preferred specific drug','It is not the preferred Cystoisospora treatment.'),('Albendazole as the routine first-line substitute','TMP-SMX is preferred; alternatives need organism-specific guidance.')],
 'Not all intestinal coccidia have the same treatment', '**Cystoisospora can produce chronic diarrhea in advanced HIV.**',['Diagnosis uses appropriate stool examination or validated molecular testing.','Relapse risk can require longer treatment or secondary prevention.'], '**Treat the parasite and restore immune function.**',['Correct dehydration and nutritional deficits.','Use specialist alternatives when TMP-SMX cannot be taken.'],[['Organism','Cystoisospora belli'],['Preferred drug','TMP-SMX'],['Long-term control','ART and relapse prevention when indicated']],[IS],style='clinical_scenario')
q('After starting ART during effective TB treatment, a patient develops enlarged tender nodes and fever despite falling HIV RNA. What is the best diagnostic principle?', 'Consider paradoxical IRIS, but first assess treatment failure, resistance, new infection and drug reactions',[
 ('The timing proves IRIS without any further evaluation','Several dangerous alternatives can look similar.'),('Stop ART permanently in every case','ART is generally continued unless a severe exceptional situation warrants specialist interruption.'),('Assume declining HIV RNA excludes all infection-related deterioration','Immune recovery can produce inflammation and coexisting infection remains possible.')],
 'Immune recovery can reveal or worsen inflammatory manifestations', '**Paradoxical IRIS worsens a previously treated infection after ART begins.**',['Unmasking IRIS presents a previously unrecognized infection.','Neither pattern is diagnosed by timing alone.'], '**Check the original treatment and competing explanations.**',['Confirm adherence and relevant microbiology.','Treat the underlying infection and assess inflammatory severity.'],[['Paradoxical','Known treated infection worsens'],['Unmasking','Previously occult infection becomes apparent'],['Exclusion','Failure, resistance, new disease and drug reaction']],[IR],style='clinical_scenario')
q('Which management principle is generally appropriate for non-life-threatening IRIS after ART initiation?', 'Continue effective ART and infection treatment, with anti-inflammatory therapy tailored to the syndrome and severity',[
 ('Stop ART routinely for every inflammatory symptom','Interruption risks HIV rebound and further complications.'),('Give high-dose steroids for every cause, including untreated Kaposi disease','Steroid risks and disease-specific contraindications matter.'),('Stop antimicrobial therapy because inflammation implies sterilization','Active infection can still require a complete course.')],
 'The treatment of IRIS is not uniform across organisms and organs', '**Mild cases may need symptomatic treatment; selected severe syndromes may need corticosteroids.**',['Cryptococcal pressure complications require their own management.','Kaposi-related inflammation requires particular caution with steroids.'], '**Use specialist input for organ-threatening disease.**',['Rare severe cases may justify ART interruption after careful assessment.','Do not confuse a general continuation principle with a prohibition on individual emergency decisions.'],[['Usually','Continue ART'],['Infection','Complete appropriate treatment'],['Inflammation','Syndrome-specific severity-based management']],[IR])
q('A patient with HIV has violaceous oral and skin lesions; biopsy shows a spindle-cell vascular neoplasm with HHV-8 evidence. What is the diagnosis?', 'Kaposi sarcoma',[
 ('Bacillary angiomatosis','It is a bacterial vascular proliferation with different histology and testing.'),('Disseminated histoplasmosis','It can mimic skin lesions but does not produce this HHV-8 spindle-cell diagnosis.'),('Primary cutaneous lymphoma','The biopsy pattern described is vascular rather than lymphoid.')],
 'Tissue distinguishes clinically similar lesions in advanced HIV', '**HHV-8 is associated with Kaposi sarcoma.**',['Assess mucosal, pulmonary and visceral involvement.','Bacillary angiomatosis is an important treatable infectious mimic.'], '**Combine effective ART with disease-stage-appropriate oncology care.**',['Advanced or symptomatic disease can require systemic therapy.','Avoid unnecessary corticosteroids because they can worsen Kaposi disease.'],[['Cause association','HHV-8'],['Tissue','Spindle-cell vascular lesion'],['Treatment','ART plus stage-appropriate tumor therapy']],[HH],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A previously suppressed patient has persistent HIV RNA above 200 copies/mL. What should precede a regimen change?', 'Assess adherence, access, interactions and treatment history, and obtain resistance testing when feasible',[
 ('Add one active drug blindly to a failing regimen','Functional monotherapy can select resistance.'),('Assume resistance is the only possible cause','Poor exposure or adherence can also cause failure.'),('Stop all drugs for months to make resistance testing easier','This risks disease progression and can obscure resistant variants.')],
 'Virological failure needs a causal evaluation', '**Review the complete treatment and resistance history.**',['Check cation interactions, food requirements and other exposure problems.','Genotyping is most informative while taking the failing regimen or promptly after stopping it.'], '**Construct a fully suppressive replacement strategy.**',['Use specialist help for complex resistance.','Address practical barriers rather than changing drugs alone.'],[['Exposure','Adherence and interactions'],['Virus','Current and historical resistance'],['New regimen','Adequate active-drug combination']],[VF],style='clinical_scenario')
q('A treated patient has one HIV RNA result of 65 copies/mL after prior suppression, followed by an undetectable repeat. What is the best interpretation?', 'An isolated viral blip rather than established persistent virological failure',[
 ('Proof that every component has become resistant','One transient low-level result does not establish that.'),('An indication to stop ART immediately','Stopping effective therapy is inappropriate.'),('Evidence that future monitoring and adherence review are unnecessary','Continued care remains important.')],
 'A transient signal differs from sustained rebound', '**Assay variation and brief biological fluctuations can produce low-level blips.**',['Confirm the trajectory and review adherence.','Persistent or rising viremia requires a different assessment.'], '**Avoid an unnecessary regimen change from one isolated result.**',['Continue appropriate monitoring.','Reassess interactions and treatment access if abnormalities recur.'],[['Isolated low value','Can be a blip'],['Repeat suppression','Supports transient event'],['Persistent rebound','Evaluate failure']],[VF,MO],style='clinical_scenario')

